(() => {
 const entries={
 'trade-capital':[
 ['Why can one bond appear in both GIRR and CSR?','Its value depends on risk-free discounting and on issuer credit spreads. These are distinct factor families even when both changes lower price.'],['Does a highlighted capital layer guarantee a positive charge?','No. It identifies risk to assess. Signed revaluation, netting, exemptions and aggregation determine the final capital outcome.'],['Why is the counterparty of an FX option not automatically a FRTB DRC issuer?','Derivative counterparty exposure is treated through counterparty credit risk and CVA rules. FRTB DRC addresses its own issuer / underlying default scope.'],['What does a barrier add to the capital map?','Path dependence can create other residual risk beyond ordinary delta, vega and curvature, subject to the RRAO scope and exclusions.'],['Why does foreign denomination add another question?','A foreign-currency value must be translated into the reporting currency. Exchange rates can change reported value even if local prices do not move.'],['Why should an option’s rate risk not be ignored?','Discounting and carry influence option value. The profile can therefore contain GIRR alongside its main underlying risk class.'],['Can a CDS and bond offset by product name alone?','No. Issuer, seniority, maturity, basis and sensitivity definitions determine whether and how offsets are permitted.'],['Why does a swaption need two maturity dimensions for GIRR vega?','Option expiry determines when exercise occurs, while underlying residual tenor describes the swap entered on exercise. Both affect volatility risk.'],['Does choosing IMA remove the need to know the SA map?','No. SA remains relevant for fallback and comparison, while IMA requires a separate eligibility and risk-factor treatment assessment.'],['What should you do after identifying a trade’s layers?','Calculate instrument-specific sensitivities and default values, map defined factors, apply valid netting and follow the class aggregation rules.']],
 'data-tests':[
 ['Can a dataset pass RFET but fail these input tests?','Yes. Real-price frequency and a faithful model history are different requirements; smoothing or poor proxy construction can still understate risk.'],['Why is a teaching tolerance adjustable?','It illustrates how an internal diagnostic responds to materiality choices. Adjusting it does not change the underlying PRA requirement or fix deficient data.'],['Can perfect correlation hide an understated risk series?','Yes. A scaled-down series can retain perfect correlation while having too little volatility and too small a tail loss.'],['Why compare four sources if they overlap?','The comparisons can reveal operational differences, but shared inputs reduce independent evidence. The analysis must explicitly account for overlap.'],['Does extrapolation need more justification than interpolation?','Yes. It goes outside supported nodes and requires the specified permission and a justified reasonable distance, rather than an invented universal distance threshold.'],['Why is issuer-specific risk easy to miss in a proxy?','A market or sector series can fit broad movements while omitting the unique shocks affecting an individual issuer.'],['Does selecting NMRF make the detected risk disappear?','No. The affected exposure requires stress-scenario treatment, and the appropriate charge must be calculated and validated.'],['Can an infrequently updated series still look statistically smooth?','Yes. Stale inputs often look calm. That appearance is not evidence of accurate current-market calibration.'],['Why is empirical stress-history justification more than a volatility ratio?','Similar amplitude alone does not establish matching risk characteristics, tail behaviour or eligibility of name-specific factors.'],['What must happen if RTPL uses the actual factor instead of its proxy?','The relevant proxy basis must be identified and adequately capitalised under the permitted exception, with the supporting evidence.']],
 'optimisation':[
 ['Does changing the counterparty prevent identical SBM factors from netting?','Not by itself. SBM factor netting is distinct from the legal counterparty netting sets used for exposure calculations.'],['Why does a different curve leave capital after an equal-size hedge?','Its rate movement is not identical. The prescribed basis correlation leaves exposure when the two curves diverge.'],['Can moving a hedge to a shorter vertex increase the charge?','Yes. The hedge can become less correlated with the primary exposure and receive a different risk weight, weakening the match.'],['Why does this experiment hold raw sensitivities fixed?','It isolates weights and aggregation. Changing a real trade’s maturity would also change its cash flows and sensitivities, requiring repricing.'],['May an unchanged issuer be remapped to a cheaper sector?','No. Bucket mapping follows actual attributes. This control changes the hedge issuer, not the classification of the original one.'],['Why might a lower-weight hedge provide less offset?','The weighted hedge becomes smaller relative to the primary weighted exposure, so a seemingly cheaper risk can be a poorer capital hedge.'],['Why does an index short not necessarily hedge every constituent?','An index combines many names. A short in one constituent only directly offsets that name under look-through.'],['Can the single-index route always use an index bucket?','No. If more than 75% of constituent weight maps to one bucket, the eligible single sensitivity is assigned there as a single-name sensitivity.'],['Why not choose an index approach separately for each trade?','The rule requires consistent treatment across instruments on the same eligible index, and moving from look-through requires the stated permission.'],['Does the smallest number in the sandbox identify the best trade?','No. It is a narrow delta calculation. Other capital layers, actual risk, liquidity, costs and portfolio objectives are outside the experiment.']]
 };
 for(const [id,questions]of Object.entries(entries)){const l=FRTB_LESSONS.find(x=>x.id===id);LEARNING_CONTENT[id]={intuition:[l.simple,'Start with the default case and change one assumption. Read the intermediate rows before comparing totals: a numerical change is useful only when you can identify the exposure, mapping or evidence that caused it.'],questions};}
})();
